  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different membranes behave in electrochemical and desalination systems, especially in seawater, and what limits their performance. The top rows compare ion‑exchange and non‑ion‑exchange membranes by showing which ions, water, and gases can pass from anode to cathode. Proton- and anion-related transport is highlighted for common membrane types such as PEM, AEM, BPM, and CEM, with arrows indicating selective movement of species like H+, OH−, Na+, Cl−, and dissolved gases. The non‑ion‑exchange examples appear more porous or less selective, allowing broader crossover of ions, water, and molecules between compartments. One panel also shows seawater separated from concentrated KOH by a bipolar-type membrane, emphasizing controlled ion transport under alkaline conditions.\n\nTaken together, the comparisons show that membrane chemistry strongly determines ion selectivity, water dissociation or transport, gas crossover, and contamination from multivalent ions. The bottom section shifts to failure mechanisms, grouping them into ion contamination and competition, chemical and mechanical degradation, and scaling/fouling. It also outlines practical mitigation strategies, including improving ion selectivity, seawater treatment, material and structural optimization, operational control, surface modification, impurity removal, and cleaning. Overall, the figure links membrane type to transport behavior, durability, and seawater compatibility.",
